@store_listing
Duskwright is a dark mode extension that never writes to the page.

Most dark-mode extensions recolor text by writing into each element's style attribute. On an app that saves inline styles into its own document — a spreadsheet, a document, a rich text editor — that styling becomes part of your file. Turn the extension off and the text is unreadable. Share the file and it is broken for whoever opens it next.

Duskwright's theming lives entirely in a layer the page cannot see or save. That is enforced by an automated test suite that blocks every release, not by a promise.

When a site cannot be themed automatically, Duskwright says so instead of failing silently, and an element picker lets you darken any region by hand. Rules you create persist per site and survive reloads. This is also the only approach that works on canvas-rendered apps, where there is no text to recolor at all.

Adjust brightness, contrast, sepia, and font preferences. Enable or disable per site, sync with system dark mode, or detect already-dark sites and leave them alone.

No ads, no analytics, no accounts, no telemetry. Duskwright requires broad site access because a dark mode extension that works everywhere needs it — the counterweight is that it sends your data nowhere, and you can verify that in the source.

Duskwright is free and open source: https://github.com/blamechris/duskwright

It is a fork of Dark Reader, used under the MIT License. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored by Dark Reader Ltd.

Please disable similar extensions to avoid conflicts.
